   <property name="text">
    <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p&gt;Konnichiwa! Welcome to our Sake brewing station!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sake is a traditional Japanese alcoholic beverage made from polished rice, water, yeast, and koji mold, which converts rice starches into fermentable sugars. Unlike beer or wine, sake occupies a unique category due to its brewing process, which more closely resembles beer production, though its alcohol content and cultural presentation align more with wine.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sake has deep historical roots in Japan, dating back over a thousand years, originally brewed in Shinto shrines and Buddhist temples for religious ceremonies and imperial court rituals. It became a symbol of purity and was often used in offerings to deities or as part of Shinto purification rites—practices that still continue in some traditional festivals and weddings today. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In modern Japan, sake remains an integral part of national identity, served during holidays like New Year's (Oshogatsu) and shared in social and ceremonial settings. While domestic consumption has declined in favor of beer and whiskey, sake has seen a global resurgence thanks to increased interest in Japanese cuisine and culture. Artisanal breweries and international competitions have elevated its status abroad, where sake is now appreciated for its nuanced flavors and versatility in food pairings.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
   </property>
